<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What a release build asks the hardened runtime for, and nothing else.

  The hardened runtime is not optional here - notarization refuses a bundle
  without it - and it takes several things away by default. Most of them Muster
  never used: it sets no DYLD_ variables, loads no library it did not sign
  itself, and generates no executable memory, so library validation, the dyld
  variables and the JIT entitlements are all absent because nothing needs them
  rather than because they were forgotten.

  The one that is here is the one a terminal cannot do without. Sending an Apple
  event from a hardened process requires it, and while the process that runs
  `osascript` in a pane is a child of the daemon rather than Muster, macOS
  charges a protected request to the responsible app - and the reason the
  Info.plist carries an NSAppleEventsUsageDescription at all.

  This file is signed onto two bundles, not one. In a bundle `dev` assembles,
  the responsible app for anything in a pane is the daemon's own helper bundle
  rather than Muster, since Launch Services starts it and it outlives every
  launch (docs/observations/macos-26.4.1.md). Muster keeps the entitlement too,
  because a plain `swift build` spawns a bare daemon and panes are charged to
  Muster there exactly as they used to be. Ghostty's own Developer ID build
  asks for the same thing, which is the closest thing to evidence available for
  a question whose failure mode is a program dying in somebody else's pane.

  Nothing in this comment may contain a double hyphen. XML forbids one inside a
  comment, and the parser that refuses it is AMFI's, inside codesign, which runs
  only when a real identity signs a bundle. `plutil -lint` accepts the file
  anyway, so the whole local gate stays green and the release is where it
  breaks: naming a flag by spelling out its dashes cost the 0.3.0 build.

  Deliberately not here: get-task-allow. That is what SwiftPM signs a debug build
  with so a debugger can attach, and it is the direct contradiction of the
  hardened runtime - a bundle carrying both is refused. The local ad-hoc path in
  `dev` keeps it; this one cannot.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
